# Knowledge base architecture

> Why this KB is shaped the way it is. Read once when joining. Update if the shape changes.

## The 10-topic frame

Every topic in `knowledge/` answers a question a new team member asks in their first 30 days:

| Topic | Question it answers |
|---|---|
| `domain_knowledge` | What is this domain? |
| `systems_and_models` | What runs in production? |
| `metrics_and_measurement` | What do we measure and why? |
| `people_and_org` | Who does what? |
| `processes_and_rituals` | How do decisions get made? |
| `gotchas_and_tips` | What will burn me? |
| `resources` | Where do I look for X? |
| `skills` | What AI/automation skills do we use? |
| `industry_landscape` | What's happening outside our team? |
| `intake` | Where do proposed additions go? |

If a question doesn't fit one of these, audit the question — usually it's a sub-question of one of the 10.

## Three artifact lifecycles

| Lifecycle | What's in it | Refresh cadence |
|---|---|---|
| **Topic** | Long-form curated content. Editorial voice. | Quarterly review |
| **Deep dive** | One-off investigation, frozen in time. | Tagged active/reference/archived |
| **Explainer** | Per-subject self-contained HTML page. | Update when subject changes |

The three should never duplicate each other. Topics summarize; deep dives investigate; explainers teach.

## What's in `auto_discovery/`

A light playbook for keeping the KB fresh: what to scan, how often, where findings land. If this KB is paired with `team-knowledge`, auto-discovery overlaps with `/brain-ingest` — pick one to be authoritative.

## Build pipeline

```
knowledge/*.md ─┐
deep_dives/*.md ├─→ build_portal.py → portal/index.html
explainers/*.html ─┘
```

Zero dependencies. Stdlib python. One file out.

## Why HTML (not a wiki engine)

- **Portable** — works on any browser, any host, offline
- **Versionable** — markdown source lives in git/drive next to other team artifacts
- **No surprises** — no engine update breaks the layout
- **Email-able** — `index.html` works as an attachment

## When to migrate away

If you outgrow the structure (>50 topics worth keeping, >100 explainers), it's time for a real CMS. This pattern is sized for teams of ~5-50 people with ~50-100 pages of durable content. Beyond that, the single-file portal stops paying off.
